WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.860
PROFESSOR: This is Susan.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.240
Susan loves to bike.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.498
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.498 --> 00:00:21.460
While out for a ride, she falls and breaks her arm.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.890
Special cells called neurons send a signal

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:29.220
through the spinal cord to the brain, which interprets the signal as pain.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.670
Susan understands the pain means she needs to go to the hospital

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.130
and her body is equipped for survival, helping her not

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.150
to panic so she can seek help.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.960
Many of her neurons are covered in proteins called opioid receptors.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.640
These receptors act like a brake to slow down the neuron&amp;#39;s ability

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.420
to send pain signals.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.460
When injured, her body releases natural painkillers called endorphins.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.850
Like a key in a lock, endorphins activate opioid receptors,

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.470
slowing down the pain signal and preventing a panic.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.940
Susan gets treated for the broken bone, but three months later her arm

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.470
still hurts.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.880
And now that pain is making her feel depressed and anxious.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.250
So her doctor prescribes an opioid painkiller.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.180
There are many different opioids, but they all share a chemical similarity

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.100
to our own endorphins.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.110
This allows them to bind to the same opioid receptors and stop pain signals,

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.510
but that&amp;#39;s not all they do.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.440
Deep inside Susan&amp;#39;s brain is a region called the ventral tegmental area,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.770
or VTA for short.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.760
The VTA is full of neurons that produce a chemical called dopamine.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.120
When something good happens, dopamine is released,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.780
giving Susan a feeling of pleasure.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.290
This helps teach your brain to keep seeking out good things.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.350
To keep dopamine neurons in check, inhibitory neurons

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.530
keep the brakes on until something good comes along.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.660
Just like the pain neuron, these neurons are covered in opioid receptors.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.080
When Susan takes the painkiller prescribed by her doctor,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.139
the opioid receptors turn off the inhibitory neurons

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.170
and release the brake on the dopamine neurons.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.710
The rush of dopamine temporarily eliminates Susan&amp;#39;s depression

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:23.400
and anxiety and she feels relief, calmness, and even euphoria.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.470
As Susan continues to take the painkillers,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.950
her brain responds by trying to regain its balance.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.070
Her inhibitory neurons work extra hard, even when

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.140
the opioid receptors are activated.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.390
And it becomes harder and harder for her dopamine neurons to release dopamine.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.330
Susan finds that she needs to increase her dose of painkillers

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.430
in order to feel comfortable.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.260
This is called tolerance.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.470
Eventually, Susan&amp;#39;s pills run out.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.900
Inhibitory neurons that have been working overtime

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.780
are let loose, clamping down on those dopamine neurons

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.570
and shutting them off almost completely.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:04.000
Now, not only is Susan in pain, but the depression and anxiety come back.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.050
On top of that, Susan feels ravaged by an inescapable physical sickness

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.600
far worse than any flu.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.230
Susan&amp;#39;s body is going through withdrawal.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.720
Most people who take opioids for a long time

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.980
tend to experience some withdrawal, but they can still stop taking the pills

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.130
and return to normal.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.870
But for people like Susan, it&amp;#39;s not so easy.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.180
Her genetics and the environment she grew up in

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.700
put her at a higher risk for addiction.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:37.370
Her withdrawal symptoms aren&amp;#39;t just unpleasant, they&amp;#39;re unbearable.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.970
Susan thinks the only way to feel normal is to find more opioids.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.940
And this is how the cycle of opioid addiction emerges, driven by a brain

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:50.300
trying to regain its balance.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.370
But there is hope for Susan.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.240
Though the road to recovery can be challenging, and there may be setbacks,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.540
treatment can retrain Susan&amp;#39;s brain.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.890
With the help of medication and therapy, Susan finds pleasure in her life

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:04.490
once again.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

